In article <HAIRSTON.90Aug28001423@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:

>[goer@quads.uchicago.edu (Richard L. Goerwitz) writes:]
>[] Has anyone else noticed that doors sometimes let you go through
>[] them diagonally, and sometimes don't?  I find the seeming incon-
>[] sistency here a bit bothersome, especially since the docs say
>[] you can only go straight through.  (Is this all in my mind?)

>it depends on "door-type" ... wouldn't it be nice if you could just
>use all doors diagonally, also (the distinctions seem strangely
>idiosyncratic).

Don't ask why they are idiosyncratic, it's just the way my mind works.. ;-)

You see, this was originally my idea. (I don't claim it is a good idea, just
stating a fact.) Yes, the reason: room floors are '.' symbol in hack. You
can move diagonally in a room (of course). Now, a doorway without a door
is also '.', so you should be able to move to it diagonally also.
(A doorway without a door looks like '.' just because this character set
is rather limited. If hack were a graphical game, I would have drawn 
a suitable symbol.)

If you still think the differences are odd, just change it back the way it
previously was (no diagonal movement as regarding doors.)

ps. I'm not sure if someone has addressed a possible bug in this. When you are
    in a shop and its door gets demolished and IF the shopkeeper didn't become
    angry at you, you could just walk out of the shop...
    (I don't know if this is at all possible.)
